s":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"In the fields of computational linguistics and probability, an n-gram is a contiguous sequence of n items from a given sequence of text or speech. The items can be phonemes, syllables, letters, words or base pairs according to the application. The n-grams typically are collected from a text or speech corpus. When the items are words, n-grams may also be called shingles.[1]  An n-gram of size 1 is referred to as a \"unigram\"; size 2 is a \"bigram\" (or, less commonly, a \"digram\"); size 3 is a \"trigram\". Larger sizes are sometimes referred to by the value of n, e.g., \"four-gram\", \"five-gram\", and so on.","topics":[],"score":213247,"global_rank":252540},{"name":"LauGds/Reeborg-s-World-Game","owner":"LauGds","html_url":"https://github.com/LauGds/Reeborg-s-World-Game","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Day-6","topics":[],"score":213239,"global_rank":252571},{"name":"LauGds/password-generator","owner":"LauGds","html_url":"https://github.com/LauGds/password-generator","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Day-5","topics":[],"score":213239,"global_rank":252572},{"name":"LauGds/rock-paper-scissors","owner":"LauGds","html_url":"https://github.com/LauGds/rock-paper-scissors","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Day-4","topics":[],"score":213239,"global_rank":252573},{"name":"LJR41/practice_dojopets","owner":"LJR41","html_url":"https://github.com/LJR41/practice_dojopets","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":213239,"global_rank":252579},{"name":"jhmartin/iptest-python","owner":"jhmartin","html_url":"https://github.com/jhmartin/iptest-python","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"DNS RR test container","topics":[],"score":213238,"global_rank":252587},{"name":"Legoben/rss2yo","ow